Your intuition is pointing at something real, but it's pointing at it sideways. Let me separate what's true from what isn't.

The part that's wrong

You cannot compress a number and get more precision from it. If you squeeze 1234567890123456 down to one digit, you get 1 — or 1.2×10^15 if you keep an exponent, which is scientific notation and gives you the same precision in less space, not more. Information can't be created by compression; only representation overhead can be removed. For a Rat that's already two coprime int64s, there's very little overhead to remove — that representation is already tight.

The other reading of your question — "use fewer bits per number so more numbers fit in memory" — doesn't help either, because RAM isn't the constraint. You have 1 MB of g_exec and 64 KB of heap for the compiler. The bottleneck is arithmetic reach, not storage.

The part that's right

There are real precision-extension tricks that fit your design, and they're library-sized, not bignum-sized. Three of them, ranked by how well they fit Frac's personality:

1. bounded.frac — adaptive denominators (the cheapest real win)

The general Rat gives you ~9 effective digits after a few multiplications, because both numerator and denominator grow. But most computations don't need arbitrary denominators — they need whatever denominator keeps the value exact-ish. If you fix the denominator at 2^k for small k, you free up 64−k bits for the numerator. At k=8, that's 56 bits ≈ 16 digits of numerator precision, versus the ~9 you get after a few general Rat operations.

That's not 2×; that's the difference between "overflows immediately" and "handles a real computation." And it's basically fix with a different grid — you already have the mechanism. A bounded.frac library that picks the denominator per-context (2^k for k in 8..32, trading generality for precision) is ~50 lines of Frac.

2. dd.frac — double-double rationals (the interesting one)

Store each number as two Rats: (hi, lo) where hi + lo is the value to ~2× precision. All arithmetic rewrites:

```
add((a,b), (c,d)) = (a+c) + (b+d)
```

Naively that's not 2× precision because a+c can lose precision that b+d was meant to capture. But the trick is error-free transforms: compute s = a+c, e = (a-s)+c, then result = (s+0) + (e+b+d). Each operation becomes 2–4× more work, but the precision roughly doubles — ~30 digits from two Rats.

Is this cheating? No — it's the exact technique behind "double-double" arithmetic (Bailey, Hida, Li), and it's how you get 106-bit precision from 53-bit IEEE doubles. Applied to rationals it's the same idea.

Is it worth it? It's the only general-arithmetic precision extension that fits in a library. It's ~200 lines of Frac, and it demonstrates something no other language can: exact rational arithmetic at ~30 digits, implemented as a library on top of a 19-digit core. That's a genuinely interesting claim.

3. cf-repr.frac — continued-fraction encoding (the clever one)

For display and comparison, continued fractions are dramatically more compact than decimals. π's CF is [3; 7, 15, 1, 292, 1, 1, 1, 2, ...] — the entry 292 is why 355/113 is such a good approximation. A CF with small entries can encode a value that would need many digits in positional notation.

But for arithmetic, CFs are awkward — a/b + c/d in CF form isn't a simple operation. So cf-repr.frac would be a library for storage and display, not for computing. You'd store, show, and compare via CF, but compute via Rats. Useful, but it doesn't extend the arithmetic ceiling.

Why none of these replace bignum

All three operate within the 64-bit-per-component ceiling. They get you 30 digits (double-double) or 16 digits per constrained computation (bounded), or compact storage (CF). They do not get you arbitrary precision, because arbitrary precision requires limbs whose count grows with the value — that's the representation changing, not the operation being clever.

The wall is specific: multiplying two 64-bit numbers produces a 128-bit result. If the numerator is already at 64 bits, the product overflows immediately. canon (gcd reduction) only helps if there's a common factor — it can't recover bits that were already lost. Any scheme that lets you keep multiplying without overflow is bignum, whether you call it bignum or not.

dd.frac's 30 digits is the maximum you get from a fixed number of limbs (specifically, 2). To get 45 digits you need 3; 60 digits, 4; and so on. At some point the bookkeeping of hi, mid, lo, ... becomes the bookkeeping of bignum limbs, and you've rebuilt bignum with extra steps.

