Why this is the answer

The calculator is the application of Frac. walk.frac is the demonstration of Frac. It's the smallest program I know that:

· Requires exact rationals (a float walk would drift and land on the wrong mediant after ~20 steps),
· Requires while/repeat (the Farey enumeration),
· Requires graphics (the tessellation),
· Requires input (arrow keys),
· Requires the compiler the user wrote,
· And visibly demonstrates a theorem (every step, the invariant holds).

It's about half the lines of the calculator and about three times as interesting, because it doesn't just compute — it shows you why the rationals are beautiful. That's the coolest possible answer to "smaller and more dynamic."

If you want it even smaller: strip the Ford drawing and just show p/q, CF, and the bracket. That's ~25 lines and still demonstrates everything. The drawing is what makes it cool, but the core is the walk, and the walk is 10 lines of arithmetic.


