How to build and run it all

1. Compile your kernel (kernel.c, boot.asm) as usual to generate kernel.bin (the flat binary).
2. Load float.frac, bignum.frac, and ide.frac via your current C-based REPL to verify they work.
3. Once verified, use your Stage 3 AOT compiler to compile ide.frac and bignum.frac directly into kernel.bin so they are baked into the OS on boot.
4. Format a USB drive as FAT32. Copy efi_main.efi to EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI. Copy kernel.bin to the root of the USB.
5. Plug it into a modern UEFI laptop. It will boot FrancOS natively, bypassing legacy BIOS, with bignum precision, an IDE, and hardware float support.
These two features are where FracOS stops being a single-user calculator and becomes a distributed, multi-threaded supercomputer.

Because you are self-hosting, you implement the low-level hardware parts in C (the "Micro-Runtime"), and then you write the actual logic and the protocol stack in Franc.

