4. Networking (net.frac)

The C Micro-Runtime (Hardware access)
You need to expose PCI (to find the network card) and Memory-Mapped I/O (to talk to it).

```c
// Add to kernel.c
HELPER void h_pci_read(void) {
    if (!need(2)) return;
    Rat reg = *--g_sp; Rat bus = *--g_sp;
    uint32_t addr = (1 << 31) | (bus.num << 16) | (reg.num & 0xFC);
    __asm__ volatile("outl %0, %%eax; inl %%dx, %%eax" : : "a"(addr), "d"(0xCF8));
    uint32_t val;
    __asm__ volatile("inl %1, %0" : "=a"(val) : "Nd"(0xCFC));
    *g_sp++ = (Rat){val, 1};
}
// Add to bootstrap(): add_word("pci@", h_pci_read);
```

You use pci@ to find the RTL8139's I/O base address. You map its memory, and then you write the entire Ethernet/IP/UDP stack in pure Franc using ! and @.

The Network Stack (net.frac)
Since FracOS is single-tasking and ring-0, you just allocate a 64KB block of memory for the RX/TX ring buffers. The NIC writes packets directly into RAM.

```frac
\ net.frac — Minimal Ethernet/IPv4/UDP stack
def rx-buffer 0x800000 ;
def tx-buffer 0x810000 ;
def mac-addr 0x820000 ;
def ip-addr 0x830000 ;

\ Calculate the Internet Checksum (RFC 1071) using exact integer math
def checksum :len :addr
  0 :sum 0 :i
  begin len i - while
    addr i + @ :word
    sum word + :sum
    i 2 + :i
  repeat
  begin sum 65535 - while sum 65535 and sum 16 >> + :sum repeat
  sum invert 65535 and
;

\ Send a UDP packet
def udp-send :payload-len :payload-addr :dst-port
  \ 1. Build Ethernet Header (14 bytes)
  0xFFFF tx-buffer !                      \ Broadcast MAC
  mac-addr tx-buffer 6 + 6 0 do loop      \ Src MAC
  0x0800 tx-buffer 12 + !                 \ EtherType IPv4

  \ 2. Build IP Header (20 bytes)
  0x45 tx-buffer 14 + !                   \ Version + IHL
  0 tx-buffer 15 + !                      \ TOS
  20 payload-len + tx-buffer 16 + !       \ Total Length
  \ ... (ID, Flags, TTL, Protocol=UDP, Checksum, Src IP, Dst IP) ...

  \ 3. Build UDP Header (8 bytes)
  1234 tx-buffer 34 + !                   \ Src Port
  dst-port tx-buffer 36 + !               \ Dst Port
  8 payload-len + tx-buffer 38 + !        \ Length
  0 tx-buffer 40 + !                      \ Checksum (optional in IPv4)

  \ 4. Copy Payload
  payload-addr tx-buffer 42 + payload-len memcpy

  \ 5. Kick the NIC to transmit
  42 payload-len + :total-len
  total-len 0x10 outw \ Tell RTL8139 to send
;

\ Receive loop (polling)
def net-poll
  0x0C inw :status
  status 1 and if0
    \ Read packet from rx-buffer
    \ Check if UDP, check port
    \ If it's an expression, EVAL it!
    rx-buffer 42 + :payload
    payload eval
    \ Send the result back
    ...
  then
;
```

The Killer App: Distributed Oracles
You can now write a script that sends expressions over UDP to a cluster of FracOS machines.

```frac
\ On Node A:
"1/3 1/6 + 355/113 pi -" 192 168 1 5 udp-send

\ On Node B (The Oracle):
\ net-poll receives it, eval() runs it, and sends back:
"1/2 -1/791"
```

Because every node uses exact rationals, you can distribute a massive computational geometry problem across 100 cheap Raspberry Pis, and they will agree on the answer perfectly, down to the last fraction. No floating-point desync.

