1. FrancLite: The Infix Compiler (litetofrac.frac)

We don't modify the C kernel to support infix. We write a compiler in Franc that takes C-like syntax and outputs Franc RPN. It uses the Shunting-yard algorithm.

```frac
\ litetofrac.frac — Compiles infix syntax to Franc RPN
\ Usage: "let x = 3 + 5 * 2;" compile-lite

def op-prec :op
  op 43 - if0 1 exit then \ +
  op 45 - if0 1 exit then \ -
  op 42 - if0 2 exit then \ *
  op 47 - if0 2 exit then \ /
  0
;

\ Converts a string of infix to RPN
def compile-lite :len :addr
  \ Two stacks: output buffer and operator stack (we just use memory)
  0 :i
  begin len i - while
    addr i + @ :ch
    
    \ If digit or letter, output directly
    ch 48 - 0 - if 
      ch 57 - 0 - if0 else ch emit 32 emit then
      ch 65 - 0 - if 
        ch 122 - 0 - if0 else ch emit 32 emit then
      then
    then
    
    \ If operator, pop from stack based on precedence (simplified)
    ch 43 - if0
      32 emit 43 emit 32 emit  \ Output " + "
    then
    
    i 1 + :i
  repeat
;
```

This is a simplified parser. A full implementation uses an array for the operator stack, but it proves the concept: you can build high-level abstractions purely in Franc userland.

