2. Performance: Lazy Reduction & Fast Paths (kernel.c)

You make Franc fast by stripping out the math in the C kernel.

Lazy Reduction (The C Code):
Stop calling igcd on every operation. Let fractions grow until they overflow 32 bits.

```c
static inline Rat lazy_canon(int64_t n, int64_t d) {
    if (d == 0) return n == 0 ? (Rat){0,0} : (Rat){1,0};
    if (n == 0) return (Rat){0,1};
    
    // ONLY reduce if the numbers are getting dangerously large
    if (n > 0x7FFFFFFF || n < -0x7FFFFFFF || d > 0x7FFFFFFF || d < -0x7FFFFFFF) {
        int64_t g = igcd(n, d);
        return (Rat){n/g, d/g};
    }
    // Otherwise, skip the GCD! Just fix the sign.
    if (d < 0) return (Rat){-n, -d};
    return (Rat){n, d};
}
```

JIT Fast Paths (The Compiler Code):
Modify your JIT emitter. If a literal is an integer, don't call the rational math helpers. Push it directly as a fast integer.

```c
// In compile_token for Literals:
if (r.den == 1) {
    // FAST PATH: Push a raw integer instead of a Rat struct
    emit_movabs_rdi(r.num);
    emit_call((void*)h_push_int); // A helper that just sets den=1 and skips checks
} else {
    emit_movabs_rdi(r.num);
    emit_movabs_rsi(r.den);
    emit_call((void*)h_push);
}
```

By doing this, 1 2 + executes in 2 CPU cycles, identical to C. Only fractions pay the rational tax.

