3. User Mode (Ring 3) and Isolation (kernel.c & syscalls.asm)

To stop an app from crashing the OS, you need hardware memory protection.

Step A: The GDT and TSS (kernel.c)

```c
// Ring 3 GDT Entries
struct gdt_entry gdt[6];
// ... setup null, kernel code, kernel data ...
// User Code (Ring 3): Access 0xFA, Flags 0xA
gdt[3] = (struct gdt_entry){0xFFFF, 0, 0, 0xFA, 0xAF};
// User Data (Ring 3): Access 0xF2, Flags 0xA
gdt[4] = (struct gdt_entry){0xFFFF, 0, 0, 0xF2, 0xAF};

// TSS (Task State Segment) is required so the CPU knows where the kernel stack is
// when an interrupt or syscall happens from Ring 3.
struct tss_entry tss;
void write_tss(int num, uint16_t ss0, uint32_t esp0) {
    // ... set up tss.rsp0 = kernel stack pointer ...
    gdt[num] = make_gdt_entry((uint32_t)&tss, sizeof(tss)-1, 0x89, 0x00);
}
```

Step B: The Syscall Interface (syscalls.asm)
The app cannot write to the screen. It must ask the kernel.

```nasm
global syscall_entry
extern syscall_handler

syscall_entry:
    ; The CPU switched to Ring 0 and set RSP to the TSS.rsp0 automatically.
    push rcx  ; Return RIP
    push r11  ; RFLAGS
    
    ; Pass arguments (RDI, RSI, RDX) to our C handler
    mov rdi, rax  ; Syscall number
    call syscall_handler
    
    pop r11
    pop rcx
    sysretq   ; Hardware fast-return to Ring 3
```

Step C: The App Lifecycle
You compile a Franc app into a flat binary. You allocate memory, mark it as User in the page tables, and jump to it via iretq (setting CS to 0x1B and SS to 0x23). If the app runs 1 0 /, it throws a Divide-by-Zero exception. The CPU sees it came from Ring 3, kills the app, and returns to the kernel prompt. FracOS is unkillable.

